( rot13-net.tal — a ROM for the hop's NET LANE: it answers over the socket, not stdout. )
(                                                                                        )
( Same transformation as rot13.tal, different lane. rot13.tal is a PIPE-lane ROM: bytes )
( arrive on the console and the answer goes to stdout, which the sh receiver hands back )
( through ssh. Shipped to a hop-serve node, that ROM would print into a terminal nobody )
( is reading and the caller would get silence — which is why hop-serve refuses any mode )
( but 'net' instead of running it and looking successful. )
(                                                                                        )
( THE LANE'S CONTRACT, and everything this ROM assumes: hop-serve has already accepted a )
( caller on CHANNEL 1 and replaced itself with these bytes. The connection survived the )
( swap because it lives in the emulator's device, not in the 64 KB that was overwritten. )
( So: read the payload from the socket, write the answer to the socket, halt. No console )
( vector, no stdin, no boot — this ROM is jumped into, not loaded. )
(                                                                                        )
( It STREAMS rather than buffering the whole payload: each chunk is transformed and )
( written back as it arrives, so the ROM has no length limit of its own and a caller )
( waiting on the first bytes is not held for the last. )
(                                                                                        )
( zero-page: tries=00 n=02 i=04 sent=06 chunk=08 )

|0100
    #03 #00 STZ                  ( three empty reads before we call it over )

@read
    #0400 #d4 DEO2
    ;buf #d6 DEO2
    #d4 DEI2 #0000 EQU2 ,&idle JCN
    #d4 DEI2 #02 STZ2
    #0000 #04 STZ2
    ;xform JMP2
    &idle
    #da DEI #00 EQU ,&done JCN   ( the caller closed: we answered everything it sent )
    #00 LDZ #01 SUB #00 STZ
    #00 LDZ #00 EQU ,&done JCN
    ;read JMP2
    &done
    #80 #0f DEO
    BRK

@xform
    &t
    ;buf #04 LDZ2 ADD2 LDA
    DUP #60 GTH ,&low JCN
    DUP #40 GTH ,&up JCN
    &put
    ;buf #04 LDZ2 ADD2 STA
    #04 LDZ2 INC2 #04 STZ2
    #04 LDZ2 #02 LDZ2 NEQ2 ,&t JCN
    ;answer JMP2
    &low
    DUP #7a GTH ,&put JCN
    #61 ;rot JSR2
    ,&put JMP
    &up
    DUP #5a GTH ,&put JCN
    #41 ;rot JSR2
    ,&put JMP

( a SHORT WRITE is normal on a socket and the device reports it, so the answer advances )
( by what actually went out; a zero-length write is a dead peer, not something to spin on )
@answer
    #0000 #06 STZ2
    &w
    #02 LDZ2 #06 LDZ2 SUB2 #08 STZ2
    #08 LDZ2 #d4 DEO2
    ;buf #06 LDZ2 ADD2 #d8 DEO2
    #d4 DEI2 #0000 EQU2 ,&dead JCN
    #06 LDZ2 #d4 DEI2 ADD2 #06 STZ2
    #06 LDZ2 #02 LDZ2 NEQ2 ,&w JCN
    #01 #00 STZ                  ( answered: one more deadline for a follow-up chunk, not )
                                 ( three — the caller has its bytes and nothing can )
                                 ( half-close, so idling three deadlines holds the port for )
                                 ( no one )
    ;read JMP2
    &dead
    #81 #0f DEO                  ( halt rc 1 — the answer did not get out, and says so )
    BRK

@rot ( c base -- c' )
    SWP OVR SUB
    #0d ADD
    DUP #19 GTH ,&wrap JCN
    &join ADD JMP2r
    &wrap #1a SUB ,&join JMP

@buf $400
